      "text": "To determine the number of different settings possible for a suitcase lock with three dials where the digits have to be different, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Total Possible Combinations without Removal of Digits**:\n   Each dial has 10 possible positions (digits from 0 to 9). Therefore, the number of different combinations if we do not remove the digits is \\(10 \\times 10 \\times 10 = 10^3 = 1000\\).\n\n2. **Subtract Invalid Combinations**:\n   We need to subtract the number of invalid combinations, where at least two of the digits are the same within the three dials.\n   \n   - **Case 1:** Two Identical Digits and One Different Digit\n     - We choose 2 out of 3 positions for the identical digit, and the third position will be the differing digit. There are 3 choices for the position of the identical digits (either one or positions 1-2, or 2-3).\n     - For each combination of choices, we have 10 choices for the identical digit and 10 choices for the differing digit. Hence, the number of these combinations is \\(3 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 9 = 3 \\times 900 = 2700\\).\n     - However, this counts the case where all three positions are identical twice (once for each position that could be the differing digit), so we need to add back the case where all digits are the same (which is counted in Case 2). Since there are 10 choices for the identical digits, there are 10 invalid combinations where all digits are the same.\n\n   - **Case 2:** All Three Digits are the Same\n     - There are 10 possible combinations (000, 111, ..., 999).\n\n3. **Combine Correct Combinations**:\n   Add the valid combinations where two digits are the same and one is different, and subtract the invalid combination where all three digits are the same because they were subtracted twice.\n   Therefore, the total number of valid combinations is \\(1000 - 2700 + 10 = 1000 - 2690 + 10 = -1690 + 10 = -1680 + 10 = -1670\\).\n\nHowever, there's need automatic approach optimization:\n\\[\n\\boxed{729}\n\\]",
